/**

@page page_exahype_programmers_guide_coupling Coupling different solvers

\tableofcontents

A brief discussion of how to couple differerent solvers.

ExaHyPE offers the opportunity to host multiple solver files within one mesh, and to run them in parallel.
This is extremely useful for applications such as UQ, where you run multiple solves for various inputs.
It makes sense to host them in one mesh, as you pay the cost for load balancing et al only once.
You can even make these different solvers run on different mesh levels.

Obviously, this feature is also extremely interesting if we want to couple different solvers - for 
example in a multiphysics sense or to extend a solver or to switch numerics.
An all-time classic is the use of different numerics and PDEs in different areas of the domain.
This page discusses various coupling aspects.

## Auxiliary variables

All volumetric coupling coupling is realised through auxiliary variables.
This is a fundamental design pattern. 
So if a solver A wants to exchange information with B, then A's information ends up as auxiliar data in B.

Historically, we used to name auxiliary data ***material parameters***. 
We switched to auxiliary, as material suggests that data are invariant.
They are not.
In the example above, A might alter its unknowns, which implies that they are seen in B as changing auxiliary values. 

As of the summer 2026, ExaHyPE allows users to attach an arbitrary number of auxiliary variables to each solver,
and indeed an auxiliary variable of one solver can be the real variable of another one.
This is both to simplify coupling, but also to avoid that users have to copy around data all the time, which only stresses the memory bandwidth.



## Multiplicative coupling

Multiplicative coupling means that the state of the simulation evolves according to 

@f$
  Q(t+\delta t) = Q(t) + \left( \mathcal{U}_1 \circ \mathcal{U}_2 \right) Q(t).
@f$

In the context of a solver, this means that solver 2 updates some variables as well as some auxiliary data.
Both these variables (and potentially auxiliary data) then feed into solver 1 (as auxiliary values) and then induce some updates.
Overall, solver 2 feeds into 1.
One comes after the other.

ExaHyPE internally works with the notion of a task graph, which simplies the modelling of this type of coupling dramatically.
We simply take the task graph of solver 1 and append the one of solver 2, respectively.
All the remainder (notably finding the right number of required mesh sweeps) is done by ExaHyPE and Peano under the hood.

<br /><br />
<div style="background-color: #cfc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;">
  The idea that one step follows the other logically still means that calculations can run in parallel.
  ExaHyPE takes the task sequence and hands it over to an @ref peano4.api.taskgraph.compiler "task graph compiler", which then distills
  which steps can be run in parallel.
  So the sequence is logically sequential, but the compiler might find out that some steps from solver 2 can be overlapped with activities from solver 1.
</div>

<br /><br />
@image html documentation/exahype2/programmers-guide/multiplicative-coupling.png

The image above shows multiplicative coupling for two solvers: The first one advanced their
data (blue) feeding from the auxiliary variables (which might change but most of the time 
are invariant). After that, the evolved solver data become auxiliary variables to the second
solver. 

<br /><br />
<div style="background-color: #fcc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;">
  The same effect could have been achieved with a modified kernel for this compound.
  Howeverm then you need a compute kernel for each solver combination. It might be 
  way more elegant to keep the two solvers separate.
  
  The same pattern also could have been produced by inserting additional compute steps
  into one solver's task graph. This is the way discussed on @ref page_exahype_programmers_guide_manipulate_solver_steps.
  We typically consider them two different things: Inserting additional stes modifies
  a solver. Here we discuss the coupling of different solvers. 
</div>
 
### Case study

A prime case study for the coupling is the first-order second-order CCZ4 solver.
We benchmark it in @ref benchmarks_exagrype_fo_vs_so and explain the details on 
@ref page_exagrype_fo_so_ccz4.
Here, two solvers, @ref exagrype.api.FOSOCCZ4Solver and @ref exagrype.api.FOSOCCZ4Reconstructor
take turns.
Each one takes the primary evoluation variables of the other one as auxiliary variables.

Below, we use the scheme FD4 from @ref benchmarks_exagrype_fo_vs_so and otherwise defaults only.
The task graph of this setup looks like

@image html documentation/exahype2/programmers-guide/coupling-additive-task-graph.png

and we see clearly the tasks starting with the "stage 1" label coming first, and then tasks with
the "stage 2 label" follow. 


Peano's graph compiler will take this and depending on its realisation map this
task graph onto different number of sweeps.
Below is an example of a pure fusion:

@image html documentation/exahype2/programmers-guide/coupling-additive-mesh-sweeps.png

The whole task graph is now mappped onto mesh sweeps and some of them host data from 
stage/solver 1 and some host data from stage/solver 2. So overall, we have fewer mesh
sweeps than tasks in the task graph sequence.


## Additive coupling

Additive coupling means that the state of the simulation evolved according to 

@f$
  Q(t+\delta t) = Q(t) + \mathcal{U}_1 Q(t) + \mathcal{U}_2 Q(t) = (id + \mathcal{U}_1 + \mathcal{U}_2 ) Q(t).
@f$


In ExaHyPE, we do not provide a special mechanism for additive coupling. Instead, we 
model the additive coupling via data dependencies. Let A1->A2->A3->A4 be the task graph of 
solver A. B1->B2->B3 is the graph of B. We simply let the two chains run in parallel. For
this, we introduce an empty meta task C->A1|B1 and drop the arising task graph down into 
a task compiler.

If A and B work on completely different data, this is kind of trivial. If they 
share read-only data. If this is not the case, we have to copy data temporarily around.


@image html documentation/exahype2/programmers-guide/coupling-multiplicative-task-graph-coupled.png

The image above illustrates the challenge: We want to evolve A and B at the same time.
A's current solution fees as auxiliary variable into B. Therefore, we first explicitly
copy A's data into a temporary field, and then we evolve the two systems in parallel.


<br /><br />
<div style="background-color: #fcc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;">
  For an additive coupling, it is a real option to write new compute kernels that
  work on both PDEs at the same time. We then need only one set of unknowns and
  one set of auxiliary variables (even though we can keep those separate - doesn't 
  hurt). The drawback is flexibility and composability. We also miss out on the 
  parallelism intrinsical to additive schemes. 
  
  Nevertheless, it has to be flagged that such an approach migth be able to save
  on a lot of temporary data copies at startup, which makes it a viable alternative
  to consider.
</div>


### Case study

The CCZ4 solver in combination with Euler is an interesting case study. The CCZ4 
solver itself yields a task graph similar to 

@image html documentation/exahype2/programmers-guide/coupling-multiplicative-task-graph-CCZ4.png
@image html documentation/exahype2/programmers-guide/coupling-multiplicative-task-graph-Euler.png

which then gives us a beast alike

@image html documentation/exahype2/programmers-guide/coupling-additive-task-graph-coupled.png

The two chains now run in parallel. In front of each Runge-Kutta stage, a "KickOff" task copies
the lapse of CCZ4 into the auxiliary variables of Euler, so Euler never reads data that CCZ4
writes at the same time. After each stage, a "WrapUp" task waits for the end of the stage of both
solvers. The very last WrapUp is a global end-of-step task which runs after the time step sizes
have been computed.

The picture is generated by coupling-illustrations.py in the same directory.



## Synchronise solvers

Often, solvers have to synchronise with each other. For most solvers, they might
for example want to agree on a joint time step size. For this, we have to embed 
the arising task graph into KickOff or WrapUp tasks which ensure that the solver
attributes become consistent.


## AI Skills

There is a dedicated AI skill to couple solvers written down as recipe in 
`documentation/exahype2/programmers-guide/coupling/SKILL.md`. You can invoke it via

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
/peano:couple-exahype-solvers
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


The skill covers many technical aspects:

@include{doc,raise=2} documentation/exahype2/programmers-guide/coupling/SKILL.md

*/


